ct module exists (android/ dir with gradle wrapper; product classes?) If not delivered: echo \"待测：模块未交付\" and exit 0.\n  - If delivered: copy test source into `android/app/src/test/java/com/osakaconnect/diagnostics/` (temp workspace), run `./gradlew testDebugUnitTest` from android/.\n  - Output log to tests/results/android-diagnostics-<date>.log? Path under tests/ allowed? Task says only change files under tests/android/android-diagnostics/. The script can write to tests/results at runtime, but that's runtime output, not committed. Fine, but safer log default to `tests/results/`? The task says \"只准改\" for files we create/commit. Runtime artifact maybe okay but avoid generating now. Script can accept OUT_LOG env or default `tests/results/android-diagnostics-ci.log`. Hmm, tests/results is not in our allowed commit scope, but script writing at CI time is not us modifying now. That's acceptable. But minimal: output to stdout and optional log. CI captures. Let's keep script writing log under `tests/android/android-diagnostics/`? Committed dir; runtime output would pollute. Better: `tests/results/`. Existing convention. I'll log there via `tee`.\n\nWait: The product code is not delivered, and the script at CI should output \"待测：模块未交付\" and exit 0. But once delivered, tests must actually fail if product wrong (not skip). The reflection approach: if classes are found, run assertions. If absent → `Assume.assumeTrue(\"待测：模块未交付\", ...)` marks skipped, gradle test task still exit 0 but reports skipped. Good — \"不能伪装通过\": skipped is visible. But requirement says \"输出（待测...）\". `Assume` message appears in skipped test report/STDOUT. With Gradle default test logging maybe not shown. We can also `println(\"待测：模块未交付\")` before assume. Alternatively use JUnit `Assume.assumeTrue`. Do both: println once + assume with same message.\n\nBut the run.sh also early-exits before gradle when android/ absent, printing \"待测：模块未交付\" exit 0. Good. That handles the current state.\n\nNow JSON parsin