know it doesn't return. Hmm. Kotlin maps Java `void fail()` to `Nothing`? Let me recall: `org.junit.Assert.fail()` is annotated? Kotlin's Java interop doesn't know it always throws. So after `if (method == null) fail(...)`, `method` remains nullable. We can use `?: throw AssertionError(\"待对齐...\")` or `?: error(...)` (IllegalStateException) — simple. Use `?: error(\"待对齐：...\")`. Fine; failure message clear.\n\nBut `assumeNoException` throws AssumptionViolatedException; after call, `error(\"unreachable\")` satisfies type (cls expression returns Class<*>). Code:\n```kotlin\nval cls: Class<*> = try { Class.forName(className) } catch (e: ClassNotFoundException) {\n    println(...)\n    assumeNoException(...)\n    error(\"unreachable\")\n}\n```\n`assumeNoException` returns Unit but always throws; code after is unreachable at runtime but compiles. Good.\n\nOwner:\n```kotlin\nval owner: Any? = if (Modifier.isStatic(method.modifiers)) null else {\n    val field = cls.declaredFields.firstOrNull { it.name == \"INSTANCE\" || it.name == \"Companion\" }\n    if (field == null) error(\"待对齐：$className.$methodName 非 static/object/companion\")\n    field.isAccessible = true\n    field.get(null)\n}\n```\n`cls.declaredFields` for Kotlin object has INSTANCE; companion has `Companion`. Accessing `Companion` instance then invoking non-static method works for `fun isValid` inside companion (not @JvmStatic)? Companion methods are instance methods on Companion class. Yes.\n\nBut if product defines `object InviteCode { fun isValid(...) }` → method non-static, owner INSTANCE. Good.\nIf it defines class with instance method `LogRedactor.redact` → no INSTANCE field, error. Product likely object. Note in header.\n\nVector helpers:\n```kotlin\nprivate fun vector(name: String): Any? {\n    var dir: File? = File(System.getProperty(\"user.dir\")).absoluteFile\n    while (dir != null) {\n        val file = File(dir, \"shared/test-vectors/$name\")\n        if (file.isFile) return Json.parse(file.readT